## The Majorelle Garden

This is the city's most famous garden. It was created by the French painter **Jacques Majorelle**, who settled in Marrakech after the First World War and gave it its intense blue, now known as "Majorelle blue". The garden was bought in 1980 by **Yves Saint Laurent and Pierre Bergé**, who saved and restored it.

It features a collection of cacti, bamboo and plants from all over the world, as well as a museum dedicated to Amazigh (Berber) cultures. The Yves Saint Laurent Museum is just a few steps away.

{% tip title="Avoiding the crowds" %}
The garden is very busy. Arrive at opening time and check the official website for ticketing arrangements before you go.
{% /tip %}

## The Menara gardens

West of the city, the Menara is laid out around a large basin and a pavilion, surrounded by olive groves. On clear days, the peaks of the Atlas rise on the horizon behind the pavilion. It is a popular walking spot with local residents, especially in the late afternoon.

## The Secret Garden

Right in the medina, the Secret Garden occupies a restored historic residence that is open to the public. It combines an Islamic garden, laid out around pools and channels, and an exotic garden. Its tower offers a view over the rooftops of the medina. It is a quiet interlude between two walks in the souks.

## The Agdal gardens

South of the medina, the Agdal gardens stretch over vast orchards of olive, orange and pomegranate trees, irrigated by large basins. Their public opening days are limited: ask locally or at your accommodation.

## The gardens around the Koutoubia

At the foot of the Koutoubia minaret, small gardens planted with orange trees and roses offer a breather just a few steps from Jemaa el-Fna square. Nearby, the Cyber Park (Arsat Moulay Abdeslam) is another shady public garden, very popular with local residents.

When you are ready to go shopping in the souks, read our tips on [haggling](/en/guides/haggling-in-the-souks).
